1. Class and Initialization:\b0\fs22\par
Let's start, there's no previous mode. So, it remembers the AC mode for the next reading, in real-world scenarios. For starters, an AC controller is created.\par
\b 2. Control Function:\b0\par
The function takes two inputs, including room temperature. What's more, whether someone is mainly in the room.\par
\b 3. Occupancy Condition:\b0\par
If nobody is there, the AC is turning OFF, and to start, the code checks if someone is in the room, on the whole.\par
\b 4. Temperature Conditions:\b0\par
- If the temperature is below 18\'b0C, the AC is changing to HEAT mode, in many respects.\par
- If the temperature is above 24\'b0C, the AC is changing to COOL mode, in actual application.\par
- Otherwise, the AC stays OFF.\par
\b 5. Verifying Previous Mode:\b0\par
The code checks if the AC is, in fact, already in the needed mode. If the mode is the same maybe, it doesn't send the command again and sends a new command, broadly speaking.\par
\b 6. Updating Previous Mode:\b0\par
The code saves the new mode so it can, to some extent, check it with the next reading after inspecting the mode, to a significant degree.\par
\b 7. Return Statement:\b0\par
In the end up, three findings, namely previous mode, new mode and are, of course, given by the code whether a command should be sent, in many respects.\b\par
8. Testing:\par
\b0 The code is tested with diverse temperatures, repeated readings, mode changes and an empty room to make sure everything works correctly, broadly speaking.\par
\b Conclusion\b0\par
This code is making AC control easier. It remembers the last mode and avoids sending the same command again and again and also makes sure that the AC turns OFF when nobody is often in the room, in many respects.\par
